          %h2#Idea Idea
          %p
            In the context of
            %em foundations of mathematics
            or
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mathematical+logic"} mathematical logic
            one studies formal systems –
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theories"} theories
            – that allow us to formalize much if not all of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mathematics"} mathematics
            (and hence, by extension, at least aspects of mathematical fields such as fundamental
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
          %p There are two different attitudes to what a desirable or interesting foundation should achieve:
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                In
                %strong
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proof+theory"} proof-theoretic
                  foundations
                the emphasis is on seeing which formal systems, however convoluted they may be conceptually, allow us to formalize and prove which
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theorems"} theorems
              %p
                The archetypical such system is
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ZFC"} ZFC
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set+theory"} set theory
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elementary+function+arithmetic"} elementary function arithmetic
                and
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/second+order+arithmetic"} second order arithmetic
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proof+theory"} proof-theoretically
                weak, and still can derive “almost all of undergraduate mathematics” (
                %a{:href => "#Harrington"}> Harrington
                ).
            %li
              %p
                In
                %strong
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/practical+foundations"} practical foundations
                (following a term introduced in (
                %a{:href => "#Taylor"}> Taylor
                )) the emphasis is on conceptually natural formalizations that
                %em concentrate the essence of practice and in turn use the result to guide practice
                (
                %a{:href => "#Lawvere"}> Lawvere
                ), as in (
                %a{:href => "#EilenbergSteenrod"}> Eilenberg-Steenrod
                ,
                = succeed ")." do
                  %a{:href => "#Harper"} Harper
              %p
                Formal systems of interest here are
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ETCS"} ETCS
                or flavors of
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+theory"} type theory
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+semantics"} categorical semantics
                and the conceptual strength of
                = succeed ")." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theory
          %p
            For a philosophical treatment of foundations see
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/foundations+and+philosophy"} foundations and philosophy
          %h2#deductive_systems Deductive systems
          %p
            Almost all
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/foundations+of+mathematics"} foundations of mathematics
            are expressed in some foundational
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/deductive+system"} deductive system
          %p
            One versatile deductive system
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+deduction"} natural deduction
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/logic+over+type+theory"} logic over type theory
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set+theories"} set theories
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+set+theories"} categorical set theories
            like
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ETCS"} ETCS
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+theory"} type theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set-level+type+theories"} set-level type theories
            as well as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+foundations"} higher foundations
            such as
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher-order+logic"} higher-order logic
          %p
            Alternatives include
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sequent+calculus"} sequent calculus
            for logic over untyped theories, such as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unsorted+set+theory"} unsorted set theory
            and untyped higher-order logic, as well as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lambda-calculus"} lambda-calculus
            for
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+theories"} type theories
          %h3#dependent_type_theory Dependent type theory
          %p
            Dependent type theory itself supports various
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/foundations+of+mathematics"} foundations of mathematics
            via the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/propositions+as+some+types"} propositions as some types
            interpretation of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type+theory"} dependent type theory
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+product+types"} dependent product types
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+sum+types"} dependent sum types
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity+types"} identity types
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+function+extensionality"} weak function extensionality
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/propositional+truncations"} propositional truncations
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/empty+type"} empty type
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unit+type"} unit type
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sum+types"} sum types
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/predicate+logic"} predicate logic
            are derivable from said type formers. Then:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                One can add a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cumulative+hierarchy"} cumulative hierarchy
                to the dependent type theory and work entirely in the cumulative hierarchy for
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/material+set+theory"} material set theory
            %li
              %p
                One can add a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+sets"} category of sets
                to the dependent type theory and work entirely in the category of sets for
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structural+set+theory"} structural set theory
            %li
              %p
                One can add a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+universe"} type universe
                satisfying certain
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axioms"} axioms
                and
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+schemata"} axiom schemata
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universe+extensionality"} universe extensionality
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/propositional+resizing"} propositional resizing
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+replacement"} replacement
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+infinity"} infinity
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+choice"} choice
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/univalent+type+theory"} univalent type theory
                or
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/univalent+foundations"} univalent foundations
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/small"} small
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/object+classifiers"} object classifiers
                as well as all
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+inductive+types"} higher inductive
                and
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coinductive+types"} coinductive types
                to the universe results in
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
            %li
              %p
                One can add a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Russell+type+of+all+propositions"} Russell type of all propositions
                and a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+numbers+type"} natural numbers type
                and work in the dependent type theory itself for
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher-order+logic"} higher-order logic
          %h2#basic_notions Basic notions
          %h3#objects_and_collections  Objects and collections
          %p In most foundations of mathematics, there are basic notions of objects and collections of objects.
          %p
            Note that in certain foundations, the objects, collections, and membership are derived notions from some other notion. Collections are referred to a number of names, such as
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/class"} class
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type"} type
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/element"} element
            or
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/term"} term
          %p
            For example, in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pure+set"} pure set
            theories like
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ZFC"} ZFC
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mostowski+set+theory"} Mostowski set theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/material+set+theory"} material set theory
            with
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/urelements"} urelements
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ZFA"} ZFA
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ETCS"} ETCS
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function"} function
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fully+formal+ETCS"} fully formal ETCS
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function"} function
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/SEAR"} SEAR
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structural+ZFC"} structural ZFC
          %p
            Something similar occurs with
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/class+theories"} class theories
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Morse-Kelley+class+theory"} Morse-Kelley class theory
            and the framework of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+set+theory"} algebraic set theory
          %p
            In type theory, there are also a distinction to be made. In most type theories, both terms and types are basic foundational notions. However, in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/book+HoTT"} book HoTT
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/types"} types
            are derived from terms.
          %p
            In
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher-order+logic"} higher-order logic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/domain+of+discourse"} domain of discourse
            and higher-order
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/predicates"} predicates
            play the role of collections, while the objects in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/domain+of+discourse"} domain of discourse
            play the role of the elements.
          %h3#membership_and_typing Membership and typing
          %p In most foundations, there is a basic notion of membership, of an “object being in a collection”.
          %p
            Foundations could be contrasted between those foundations where membership is a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/membership+relation"} membership relation
            = succeed "." do
              %span.newWikiWord
                typing judgment
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/typing+judgment"} ?
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unsorted+set+theories"} unsorted set theories
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simply+sorted+set+theories"} simply sorted set theories
            on one hand, and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependently+sorted+set+theories"} dependently sorted set theories
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type+theories"} dependent type theories
            on the other hand.
          %p
            For theories with a membership relation, one could further distinguish between
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homogeneous+membership+relations"} homogeneous membership relations
            (for relations on the same sort) and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/heterogeneous+membership+relations"} heterogeneous membership relations
            (for relations between two different sorts), as well as membership relations which are primitive to the theory, and membership relations which are derived from some other existing structure in the theory.
          %p
            For theories with a typing judgment, one simply judges the object, element, or term
            %span.mathematics a
            to be in the collection, set, or type
            = succeed "," do
              %span.mathematics A
            = succeed "." do
              %span.mathematics a:A
            %span.mathematics a
            is in collection, set, or type
            %span.mathematics A
            is true or not.
          %p
            Formally, there are also a difference in the contexts. When () or
            %span.mathematics a:A
            is a judgment, then it lies in the type layer, but when () is a true proposition, then it lies in the proposition layer
            %span.mathematics Φ
            while the typing judgment
            = succeed "Set" do
              %span.mathematics a:
          %h3#equality_and_equivalence Equality and equivalence
          %p
            In most foundations such as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type+theory"} dependent type theory
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/logic+over+type+theory"} logic over type theory
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equality"} equality
            or
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence"} equivalence
            of the terms of the language.
          %p
            There are broadly three different notions of equality which could be distinguished:
            = succeed "," do
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/judgmental+equality"} judgmental equality
            = succeed "," do
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/propositional+equality"} propositional equality
            = succeed "," do
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/typal+equality"} typal equality
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/typed+predicate+logic"} typed predicate logic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity+type"} identity type
            or
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/path+type"} path type
            for elements, and the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+of+equivalences"} type of equivalences
            for types.
          %p
            The equality of elements used in most
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set+theories"} set theories
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/class+theories"} class theories
            are
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/propositional+equalities"} propositional equalities
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type+theories"} dependent type theories
            are
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/typal+equality"} typal equality
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/objective+type+theory"} objective type theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/judgmental+equality"} judgmental equality
            or
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/propositional+equality"} propositional equality
            to use for
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/definitions"} definitions
          %p
            The equality of collections differs from theory to theory. There are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set+theories"} set theories
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/class+theories"} class theories
            where
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/propositional+equality"} propositional equality
            is used as the notion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equality"} equality
            between sets and classes, while for other set theories and class theories, the notion of equality between sets and classes is rather
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bijection"} bijection
            of sets and classes, rather than equality of sets and classes. Something similar happens in dependent type theory, depending on whether one uses a separate type judgment, or
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Russell+universes"} Russell universes
            without a separate type judgment: for Russell universes, the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity+type"} identity type
            is used for the equality of types in the universe, since types are terms of Russell universes. On the other hand, the dependent type theories with a separate type judgment, there is no identity type between two types, and one has to use the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+of+equivalences"} type of equivalences
            instead.
          %p
            A mathematical foundation is said to respect the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principle+of+equivalence"} principle of equivalence
            if there is no strict
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/propositional+equality"} propositional equality
            between sets. Thus, any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unsorted+set+theory"} unsorted set theory
            like
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fully+formal+ETCS"} fully formal ETCS
            violates the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principle+of+equivalence"} principle of equivalence
          %h3#size_issues_universes_and_classes Size issues, universes, and classes
          %p
            In most foundations of mathematics, there is no way to talk about the collection of all collections; this is known as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/size+issues"} size issues
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mathematics"} mathematics
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universes"} universes
            = succeed "," do
              %span.mathematics U
            %strong
              %span.mathematics> U
              \-
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/small+sets"} small sets
            or
            = succeed "," do
              %strong
                %span.mathematics> U
                \-small types
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subsets"} subsets
            or
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subtypes"} subtypes
            are called
            = succeed "." do
              %strong
                %span.mathematics> U
                \-
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classes"} classes
            %span.mathematics U
            are called
            %strong
              %span.mathematics> U
              \-
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/large+sets"} large sets
            or
            = succeed "," do
              %strong
                %span.mathematics> U
                \-large types
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subsets"} subsets
            or
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subtypes"} subtypes
            which are not
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/singleton+subsets"} singleton subsets
            or subtypes are called
            = succeed "." do
              %strong
                %span.mathematics> U
                \-
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proper+classes"} proper classes
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universes"} universes
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                In
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set+theory"} set theory
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+universes"} Grothendieck universes
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set+theory"} set theory
                or
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/class+theory"} class theory
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/well-pointed+category"} well-pointed
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Heyting+categories"} Heyting categories
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/well-pointed+category"} well-pointed
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/division+allegories"} division allegories
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sets"} sets
                with
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extensional+relation"} extensional
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/well-founded+relation"} well-founded
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/transitive+relation"} transitive
                relations,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categories+with+class+structure"} categories with class structure
            %li
              %p
                In
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/class+theory"} class theory
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+class"} universal class
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/class"} class
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universe"} universe
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classes"} classes
                = succeed "-small" do
                  %span.mathematics U
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sets"} sets
            %li
              %p
                In
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type+theory"} dependent type theory
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/univalent+universe"} univalent
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Russell+universes"} Russell universes
                or
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tarski+universes"} Tarski universes
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type+theory"} dependent type theory
          %h2#Concrete Effect of foundations on concrete problems
          %p It may seem on first sight that foundational questions in mathematics are remote from “real mathematics”. This is not quite so. For a list of “real world” problems that do depend on foundations see
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/effects+of+foundations+on+%22real%22+mathematics"} effects of foundations on "real" mathematics
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/New+Foundations"} New Foundations
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/univalent+foundations+for+mathematics"} univalent foundations for mathematics
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/synthetic+mathematics"} synthetic mathematics
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory+and+foundations"} category theory and foundations
          %p
          %h2#section_Philosophical_musings Philosophical musings
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gravity+and+grace"}> Gravity and grace
              , Simone Weil.
          %h2#references References
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bertrand+Russell"}> Bertrand Russell
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Principia+Mathematica"} Principia Mathematica
            %li#Lawvere
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/William+Lawvere"}> William Lawvere
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Foundations and applications: axiomatization and education
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.math.ucla.edu/~asl/bsl/0902/0902-006.ps"} ps
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://projecteuclid.org/euclid.bsl/1052669290"} Euclid
            %li#Harrington
              %p
                L. A. Harrington (ed.),
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Harvey Friedman’s Research on the Foundations of Mathematics
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Stefania+Centrone"}> Stefania Centrone
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Deborah+Kant"} Deborah Kant
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Deniz+Sarikaya"} Deniz Sarikaya
                (editors),
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Reflections+on+the+Foundations+of+Mathematics"} Reflections on the Foundations of Mathematics
                %strong 407
                Springer (2019) &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-030-15655-8"}> doi:10.1007/978-3-030-15655-8
                \&amp;rbrack;
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/practical+foundations"} Practical foundations
            in terms of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+theory"} type theory
            language are laid out in
          %ul#Taylor
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Paul+Taylor"}> Paul Taylor
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Practical+Foundations+of+Mathematics"} Practical Foundations of Mathematics
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://www.paultaylor.eu/~pt/prafm/index.html"} web
          %p
            Under
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/computational+trinitarianism"} computational trinitarianism
            this corresponds to a practical foundation in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/program"} programming
          %ul#Harper
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Robert+Harper"}> Robert Harper
              ,
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Practical+Foundations+for+Programming+Languages"} Practical Foundations for Programming Languages
              (
              %a{:href => "https://www.cs.cmu.edu/~rwh/pfpl/"}> web
              )
          %p
            A classification of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/material+set+theories"} material set theories
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structural+set+theories"} structural set theories
            is in
          %ul#Shulman2018
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michael+Shulman"} Michael Shulman
              (2018). Comparing material and structural set theories.
              = succeed "." do
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1808.05204"} arXiv:1808.05204
          %p
            A foundation for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+topology"} algebraic topology
            in this practical spirit is laid out in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Samuel+Eilenberg"}> Samuel Eilenberg
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Norman+Steenrod"} Norman Steenrod
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Foundations+of+Algebraic+Topology"} Foundations of Algebraic Topology
          %p A big picture intro to the comparison between set theory, type theory and topos/category theory as approaches to foundations is in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Steve+Awodey"}> Steve Awodey
              ,
              %em From Sets to Types to Categories to Sets
              %a{:href => "http://www.andrew.cmu.edu/user/awodey/preprints/stcsFinal.pdf"} pdf
          %p A comparative discussion of complexities of different foundations is in
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Freek+Wiedijk"}> Freek Wiedijk
                ,
                %em Is ZF a hack? Comparing the complexity of some (formalist interpretations of) foundational systems for mathematics
                (
                %a{:href => "http://www.cs.ru.nl/~freek/zfc-etc/zfc-etc.pdf"}> pdf
                )
              %p
                %strong Abstract
                This paper presents
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Automath"} Automath
                encodings (which also are valid in LF/P) of various kinds of foundations of mathematics. Then it compares these encodings according to their size, to find out which foundation is the simplest.
              %p
                The systems analyzed in this way are two kinds of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set+theory"} set theory
                (ZFC and NF), two systems based on Church’s
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+order+logic"} higher order logic
                (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Isabelle"}> Isabelle
                \/Pure and
                = succeed ")," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/HOL"} HOL
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+theory"} type theory
                (the
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/calculus+of+constructions"} calculus of constructions
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ETCS"} foundation based on category theory
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Colin+McLarty"}> Colin McLarty
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Set theory for Grothendieck’s number theory
                %a{:href => "http://www.cwru.edu/artsci/phil/Groth%20found.pdf"} pdf
          .property
